cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this material, please cite the archived Zenodo record rather than the repository URL."
title: "A finite range where the SubexponentialSAT bound's exponential factor sits above 2^n with A = 1, and the receipt of a comparator rerun"
abstract: >-
  Two checkable things about the SubexponentialSAT challenge of openai/math. First, the receipt of
  a rerun of leanprover's comparator on the solution, under a real sandbox, with the
  three limits of that run stated and the raw log included. That run used Lean's default kernel, so
  it is not a check by a second kernel implementation. Second, a recomputation of the stated
  time bound: taking A = 1, the ratio runtimeScale(n)/n is not monotone, so the exponential factor
  sits above 2^n over a finite range of n. Both crossings of that range are closed-form in the
  Lambert W function, at n = 49.175096 on the principal branch and near 10^2390.21 on branch -1,
  with the maximum 16/e^2 at L = e^4. The finite range has no asymptotic consequence, and this
  material refutes nothing: it neither establishes that the formal statement is a faithful
  rendering of k-SAT nor that the unary encoding keeps the input size polynomial.
